+    // AND THE FIRST WALK OF THE WINDOW TREE, IF IT HAS NOT HAPPENED YET.
+    //
+    // viewDidAppear: was meant to be that: whatever UIKit could answer during
+    // startup, by there it answers for real, and the walk repairs any window
+    // that stored a colour before then. But this port defers OnInit off the
+    // launch, and wx turns the run loop while the frame is being built, so
+    // viewDidAppear: can arrive while GUI_App::mainframe is still null. There
+    // is nothing to walk at that point, and orca_ios_appearance_changed()
+    // deliberately does not latch when it finds no frame - but nothing was
+    // calling it again either. The next thing to touch the colours was some
+    // unrelated later update, which is exactly the "it arrives after a while
